WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.289 --> 00:00:05.634
Hi, I'm Ronnie. Years of swimming taught me to notice how small differences in movement

2
00:00:05.654 --> 00:00:11.188
can affect control, efficiency, and performance.

3
00:00:11.208 --> 00:00:17.646
That curiosity led me to study the Functional Movement Screen, and eventually to build AI-FMS.

4
00:00:17.721 --> 00:00:21.054
FMS stands for Functional Movement Screen.

5
00:00:21.073 --> 00:00:26.231
It is a standardized screening system that uses seven fundamental movement patterns

6
00:00:26.251 --> 00:00:32.143
to observe how mobility, stability, symmetry, and motor control work together.

7
00:00:32.163 --> 00:00:34.880
Each pattern is scored from zero to three.

8
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.154
It is a movement screen, not a medical diagnosis.

9
00:00:38.335 --> 00:00:41.737
Before evaluating other people, I wanted to understand

10
00:00:41.757 --> 00:00:44.881
the protocol responsibly.

11
00:00:44.991 --> 00:00:50.922
I completed FMS Level 1 and Level 2 training and earned both certifications.

12
00:00:50.942 --> 00:00:56.477
The training gave me a structured foundation for setting up the seven screens,

13
00:00:56.496 --> 00:01:01.000
applying the scoring rules, and recognizing when a result requires further human attention.

14
00:01:01.198 --> 00:01:07.327
After the training, I began conducting and reviewing FMS assessments.

15
00:01:07.347 --> 00:01:09.460
I saw four practical limitations.

16
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.196
Movements pass quickly.

17
00:01:11.216 --> 00:01:14.618
Remote review makes repetitions slow to find.

18
00:01:14.638 --> 00:01:19.676
Visual judgment remains qualitative even when angles and trajectories matter,

19
00:01:19.696 --> 00:01:24.606
and the zero-to-three score preserves little of the evidence behind the decision.

20
00:01:24.821 --> 00:01:28.144
Those problems became the starting point for AI-FMS.

21
00:01:28.184 --> 00:01:33.362
AI-FMS is an explainable, human-in-the-loop video-review system.

22
00:01:33.381 --> 00:01:39.990
The workbench supports all seven FMS movements, with repetition-level review

23
00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.947
and a movement-specific, pose-based first-pass suggestion.

24
00:01:42.966 --> 00:01:48.736
The AI can explain its evidence and abstain when video quality or protocol information is incomplete.

25
00:01:48.781 --> 00:01:58.394
A reviewer can isolate complete repetitions, replay a difficult moment, and correct the timing

26
00:01:58.414 --> 00:02:03.830
instead of searching through the full video again. The MediaPipe overlay is aligned to the video,

27
00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:11.806
allowing the system to preserve angles, normalized distances, trajectories, and movement events that are hard to record consistently by eye.

28
00:02:11.826 --> 00:02:16.479
The AI suggestion supports, rather than replaces, reviewer judgment.

29
00:02:16.499 --> 00:02:21.868
It shows which criteria support a score, what remains uncertain,

30
00:02:21.887 --> 00:02:23.823
and whether protocol information is missing.

31
00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:29.831
The reviewer records the final score, confidence, camera view, side, clearing information,

32
00:02:29.851 --> 00:02:30.423
and notes.

33
00:02:30.473 --> 00:02:36.604
To evaluate the system without leaking AI information, we built a separate Study Mode.

34
00:02:36.624 --> 00:02:42.565
In both blinded rounds, reviewers could not see AI scores, pose parameters, source file names,

35
00:02:42.585 --> 00:02:47.524
previous answers, or the other reviewer's decisions.

36
00:02:47.544 --> 00:02:52.361
Reviews were stored as append-only events and exported with checksums.

37
00:02:52.426 --> 00:02:57.762
Phase I reconstructed 110 repetitions from 28 source videos

38
00:02:57.782 --> 00:03:04.408
and selected 32 across four movements for two blinded rounds.

39
00:03:04.427 --> 00:03:12.045
In Round B, the reviewers agreed on scoreability for all 32 items and assigned the same score to all 26 jointly scoreable items.

40
00:03:12.065 --> 00:03:19.038
The locked AI exactly matched human consensus on 16 of 25 comparable items

41
00:03:19.058 --> 00:03:24.711
and was within one point on 23. This is an internal benchmark, not held-out clinical validation.

42
00:03:24.761 --> 00:03:30.593
The quantitative evidence also revealed what the ordinal score does not show. Deep Squat formed a strategy continuum;

43
00:03:30.613 --> 00:03:36.644
ASLR preserved side and repeatability differences;

44
00:03:36.663 --> 00:03:42.595
Hurdle Step showed several pathways to the same score;

45
00:03:42.615 --> 00:03:48.516
and Rotary Stability required a full-cycle view of coordination and event sequence.

46
00:03:50.461 --> 00:03:55.261
My role in this project grew far beyond demonstrating a piece of software.

47
00:03:55.559 --> 00:04:01.530
I worked through an AI-assisted development process, but the project taught me that responsible technology

48
00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:07.025
depends on human judgment, data quality, and the willingness to say when the evidence is not enough.

49
00:04:09.286 --> 00:04:15.446
In college, I hope to continue exploring Human Movement Science, biomechanics, and responsible AI.

50
00:04:15.783 --> 00:04:19.235
For me, AI-FMS is not the end of a project.

51
00:04:19.413 --> 00:04:22.925
It is the beginning of a research direction I want to keep pursuing.
